{"author":{"id":"4181bb786fe738a498a0c626b33a338f446d838be54c2aa6c43039367353685b"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1737381131,"nonce":"rTKzfJjc5vAcQX0doPjbEeM7D9A=","metadata":{"github-id":"I_kwDOAdQ_c86m2mIr","github-url":"https://github.com/Xe/x/issues/651","origin":"github"},"title":"cmd/anubis: Some ideas (support non JS users, forward auth and blocking known bots)","message":"Hello,\n\nFirst I would like to emphasize that I have been working on the same path as anubis on my public service (https://xcancel.com), a project named [antibot-proxy](https://github.com/unixfox/antibot-proxy) and later on giving the ideas for [the antibot](https://github.com/searxng/botdetection) in [SearXNG](https://github.com/searxng). And I have been interested in anti bot solutions for 8 years.\n\n### 1st idea: Fallback for non JS users\n\nMost antibot solution fails to cover the fact that a minority of Internet users have JS disabled. For whatever reason some users are disabling JS, I won't go into details here, if you want to understand more type in your search engine \"why disabling javascript privacy\".\n\nUnfortunately, this is the case of anubis. It's sad to straight block them, at least giving them a \"fallback\" choice would be a good idea.\n\nThere are multiples ways to achieve that:\n\n#### Easy ones to implement\n\n- Offer an image based CAPTCHA. Even though it's cumbersome to the user, at least the user is not entirely blocked from visiting the website.\n  Your CAPTCHA can be a simple image, or [an image generated from CSS (harder to extract for using in anti CAPTCHA solution)](https://github.com/javierbyte/img2css)\n- Detect the usage of a real browser by checking if the client can load CSS, images files. [A while ago, I created a project like this](https://github.com/unixfox/antibot-proxy)\n- If the client sends normal headers that a browser sends: [What HTTP Headers is my browser sending?](https://www.whatismybrowser.com/detect/what-http-headers-is-my-browser-sending/), [filtron](https://github.com/asciimoo/filtron)\n\n#### Harder ones to implement\n\n- Detect if it's a browser by using some CSS properties. [Example 1](https://codepen.io/mehranbolhasani/pen/WNGrJpQ), [Example 2](https://noscriptfingerprint.com/), [Example 3](https://csstracking.dev/), [Example 4](https://gist.github.com/Abdullah-FAl/43a8d77ea10675ee67bf41269fe51f74) and [Example 5](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=38095699)\n  CSS tracking stuff is just for demo, can reuse the CSS properties in a good way for just browser checking and not for fingerprinting the users.\n\n#### And some quirky ones\n\n- Using Etag to detect browser usage: https://levelup.gitconnected.com/no-cookies-no-problem-using-etags-for-user-tracking-3e745544176b?gi=0aa90272c90e\n- Detect browser by interpreting the HTML content: [meta refresh](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meta_refresh)\n- Old headers that browsers still accept but [not conventional automated libraries](https://github.com/curl/curl/issues/3657): [Refresh header](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Headers/Refresh)\n- Don't load all the HTML (using Transfer-Encoding: chunked) and detect if the client reacts like a browser by sending resources (CSS, images) bits by bits over time: [CSS Only Chat](https://github.com/kkuchta/css-only-chat)\n\n### 2nd idea: Forward auth\n\nAt the current state, anubis acts like the central hub between the reverse proxy and the final app. It has to process all the whole HTTP packets transmitted between the reverse proxy and the final app.\n\nThis has some direct effects: less efficient than a reverse proxy, increase in internal bandwidth usage, increase in CPU usage, increase in memory usage, slows down a bit the user experience since the HTTP packets have to be processed by 3 applications.\n\nIn most major reverse proxy [NGINX](https://nginx.org/en/docs/http/ngx_http_auth_request_module.html), [Caddy](https://caddyserver.com/docs/caddyfile/directives/forward_auth), [Traefik](https://doc.traefik.io/traefik/middlewares/http/forwardauth/). You have what's called a forward auth plugin.\n\nWhen the reverse proxy receives an HTTP request, it sends the HTTP headers and the path to an external application for what to do, blocking the request or passing the request to the final backend.\n\nThis has the benefit of not having the entire HTTP request being transmitted through the external app, and you can keep using all the bells and whistles of your preferred reverse proxy (blue-green scenario, canary scenario, load balancing and so on).\n\nanubis could be modified to be used this way instead. The verification page would be on a separate path, to which the user is redirected to when anubis has never seen him. The browser do the JS challenge and if it passes, then it is redirected to the original page.\n\nThe downside is that this might cause some issue with POST requests, but I don't have a solution for that right now. They are probably some solutions for that.\n\n### 3rd idea: Use TLS/HTTP2 fingerprint to block known bots using automated libraries (go, python, ...) and faking their user agent to pretend to be a browser\n\nHere is an explanation on how TLS fingerprinting works: https://engineering.salesforce.com/tls-fingerprinting-with-ja3-and-ja3s-247362855967/ and https://fingerprint.com/blog/what-is-tls-fingerprinting-transport-layer-security/\n\nThere are not that many public JA3 databases but since the creation of [JA4](https://github.com/FoxIO-LLC/ja4/blob/main/technical_details/JA4.md), there is a public one available: https://ja4db.com/\n\nYou can also validate if it's a real browser based on their [HTTP2 fingerprint](https://browserleaks.com/http2). There are no known databases for that, but usually it's a fixed fingerprint for all Chrome browsers of a specific version range or all Firefox browsers of a specific version range.\n\nHTTP2 and JA3/JA4 fingerprinting require using a modified reverse proxy: [JA4 see Tools that support JA4+](https://github.com/FoxIO-LLC/ja4), [JA3 NGINX](https://github.com/fooinha/nginx-ssl-ja3), [HTTP2 NGINX](https://github.com/phuslu/nginx-ssl-fingerprint), [JA4 Haproxy](https://github.com/O-X-L/haproxy-ja4), [JA3 haproxy](https://github.com/O-X-L/haproxy-ja3n/)\n\n---------------\n\nI know it's a long issue, but feel free to split into smaller issues if you want to implement some ideas with what solution you prefer.\n\nI can provide more answer if you didn't understand some things that I explained here. It's a summary of everything I have noted over the years if I still had time to create an open source solution that is a good alternative to using something like Cloudflare but better.","files":null}]}